Title: Kittens, MOGs, and Mathematical Blackjack
Episode: 2. The Geometry of a Kitten
Credit: Written by
Author: David Joyner, Claude (Anthropic) and ChatGPT (OpenAI)
Source: ep2/outline.json; the mathematics is checked against math_objects.json
Draft date: 2026-11-28
Notes: Draft 1. Each beat of the outline is named by a [[beat: sNN.bMM]] note on the action that opens it. Each dialogue line carries a stable [[line: sNN.bMM.lKK]] note; the audio, captions and cues.json refer to lines by these IDs. Never renumber a line: add a suffix (s03.b02.l01a) instead. Numbers are written as words in dialogue so they are spoken as words. Spoken respellings (Euler as "Oiler", Fano as "Fah-no") live in pronunciation.json, not here.

[[ PRODUCTION NOTE: The same tabletop as Episode 1, seen from the front, with the kittens in the same seats: Sophie far left, Pascal left of center, Ada right of center, Euler far right. The cards are put away. Sophie still has the chalk she found at the end of Episode 1, and every diagram is drawn with it in the dark space above the table. Points are chalk dots with their labels; from Curtis's kitten on, points are drawn as small cards, as in the titles. ]]

# s00. Main title

[[ The series main title and the Episode 2 subtitle play here (titles/outline.json; scene s00). No dialogue. ]]

# s01. Special how?

INT. KITCHEN TABLE - EVENING

[[#1 | BEAT: Special how? | OBJ: Sophie wants a picture of the losing hands | SETUP: none]]

The losing hands from last time line up again across the table: 1 to 6; the 21 that ended the first game, 0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7; Ada's 24, 0, 1, 2, 4, 7, 10; and the 45-hand, 0, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, with its question mark. Sophie holds her chalk. [[beat: s01.b01]]

SOPHIE
Last time, these hands lost. One to six. The end of our first game. Ada's twenty-four. And forty-five, which we only tested. [[line: s01.b01.l01]]

SOPHIE
They look special. But special how? [[line: s01.b01.l02]]

PASCAL
They lost. That's how they're special. [[line: s01.b01.l03]]

Sophie raises the chalk.

SOPHIE
Losers with a secret. I need a picture. [[line: s01.b01.l04]]

# s02. Seven points

[[#2 | BEAT: Seven points | OBJ: Sophie shows a geometry small enough to hold | SETUP: none]]

The hands clear away. Sophie draws seven chalk dots, numbered 1 to 7, then three sides of a triangle, three lines through the middle, and a circle. [[beat: s02.b01]]

SOPHIE
Here's a tiny geometry, the Fano plane. Seven points and seven lines. [[line: s02.b01.l01]]

ADA
Only seven points? [[line: s02.b01.l02]]

SOPHIE
Only seven. A finite geometry: there's nothing between the points. [[line: s02.b01.l03]]

Sophie taps two dots with the chalk, 1 and 4. [[beat: s02.b02]]

SOPHIE
The rule is, any two points lie on exactly one line. Pick one and four. [[line: s02.b02.l01]]

A pause for the viewer: which point completes their line?

The line through 1 and 4 lights up, and it calls up its third point, 2.

SOPHIE
Their line calls up a third point: two. [[line: s02.b02.l02]]

Sophie taps 4 and 5. [[beat: s02.b03]]

SOPHIE
Now four and five. [[line: s02.b03.l01]]

The circle lights up and calls up 6.

SOPHIE
Their line is the circle, and it brings six. [[line: s02.b03.l02]]

PASCAL
(objecting)
Hold on. A circle isn't a line. [[line: s02.b03.l03]]

SOPHIE
It has three points, and it obeys the rule. Here, that makes it a line. [[line: s02.b03.l04]]

EULER
In geometry, a line is whatever obeys the rules. [[line: s02.b03.l05]]

# s03. Twelve points, five at a time

[[#3 | BEAT: Twelve points, five at a time | OBJ: Sophie asks for a bigger design and counts it before she has it | SETUP: none]]

Beside the Fano plane, its name builds up from its parts: 2 points, 3 on a line, 7 in all. S(2,3,7). [[beat: s03.b01]]

SOPHIE
Every two of the seven points lie on exactly one line of three. That's a Steiner system, S(2,3,7). [[line: s03.b01.l01]]

The Fano plane fades. Twelve blank dots stand in a ring. Five light up, and a sixth waits with a question mark. [[beat: s03.b02]]

SOPHIE
Now I want twelve points, and blocks of six, so that every five points lie in exactly one block. An S(5,6,12). [[line: s03.b02.l01]]

EULER
They call that the small Witt design. [[line: s03.b02.l02]]

ADA
Before you draw it: how many blocks? [[line: s03.b02.l03]]

The count builds up beside the ring. [[beat: s03.b03]]

SOPHIE
Count the fives first. Twelve points, five at a time: seven hundred ninety-two ways. [[line: s03.b03.l01]]

SOPHIE
A block of six holds six of those fives, and no five can be in two blocks. [[line: s03.b03.l02]]

SOPHIE
So seven hundred ninety-two over six. A hundred thirty-two blocks. [[line: s03.b03.l03]]

Sophie writes a name over the waiting block. [[beat: s03.b04]]

SOPHIE
We'll call each block a hexad. [[line: s03.b04.l01]]

ADA
(skeptical)
If such a thing exists. Counting it doesn't make it real. [[line: s03.b04.l02]]

# s04. Two moves on a ring

[[#4 | BEAT: Two moves on a ring | OBJ: Euler builds the design and checks it completely | SETUP: none]]

Euler takes the ring. Eleven of the dots become a clock, 0 to 10, and the twelfth slides into the middle as infinity. [[beat: s04.b01]]

EULER
Let me try. Zero to ten around a clock, and infinity in the middle. [[line: s04.b01.l01]]

A 10 glows, and one step on, the 0.

EULER
On this clock, ten plus one is zero again. That's arithmetic mod eleven. [[line: s04.b01.l02]]

EULER
Add infinity, and mathematicians call it the projective line. [[line: s04.b01.l03]]

Six points light up on the ring: 0, 1, 3, 4, 5 and 9. [[beat: s04.b02]]

EULER
For a seed, take zero and the nonzero squares: one, four, nine, five and three. [[line: s04.b02.l01]]

SOPHIE
Six points. Our first hexad? [[line: s04.b02.l02]]

The six marks step once around the clock. [[beat: s04.b03]]

EULER
Move one: add one. Every point steps around the clock, and infinity stays put. [[line: s04.b03.l01]]

The marks return to the seed, then trade places across the ring.

EULER
Move two: the negative reciprocal. Zero and infinity trade places, and so do two and five. [[line: s04.b03.l02]]

EULER
Each move is a permutation. It only rearranges the twelve labels. [[line: s04.b03.l03]]

Euler sets both moves loose. New six-point sets pile up, layer by layer. [[beat: s04.b04]]

EULER
Now apply both moves to every new set, again and again. [[line: s04.b04.l01]]

The count climbs: 3, 6, 11, 19, 31, 48, 73, 102, 120, 128, 131, 132, and stops.

SOPHIE
It stopped. [[line: s04.b04.l02]]

EULER
A hundred thirty-two. Everything the moves can reach from the seed is called its orbit. [[line: s04.b04.l03]]

Each of the 132 sets breaks into its six fives. [[beat: s04.b05]]

EULER
Now break every set into its fives. Seven hundred ninety-two fives, and each one appears exactly once. [[line: s04.b05.l01]]

Sophie marks five points on the ring: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

SOPHIE
So any five points, like one, two, three, four, five... [[line: s04.b05.l02]]

The sixth point answers: 7.

EULER
...call up exactly one more. Seven. [[line: s04.b05.l03]]

ADA
(nodding)
All right. It exists. [[line: s04.b05.l04]]

# s05. Curtis's kitten

[[#5 | BEAT: Curtis's kitten | OBJ: Sophie draws the same twelve points as a picture | SETUP: none]]

The ring's labels fly into a triangle of small cards, corners infinity, 0 and 1 in gold. [[beat: s05.b01]]

SOPHIE
Here's another picture of the same twelve points. The mathematician Curtis called it the kitten. [[line: s05.b01.l01]]

PASCAL
It's a triangle. [[line: s05.b01.l02]]

SOPHIE
A triangle with three corners: infinity, zero and one. [[line: s05.b01.l03]]

The three copies of 6 glow. [[beat: s05.b02]]

SOPHIE
Some labels appear three times. Three sixes, but only one point. [[line: s05.b02.l01]]

The triangle comes apart. At each corner, the other nine points gather into a three-by-three grid. [[beat: s05.b03]]

SOPHIE
Seen from each corner, the other nine points make a three-by-three grid. It's called an affine plane. [[line: s05.b03.l01]]

In the grid at infinity, a row lights up, then a column, then a diagonal. [[beat: s05.b04]]

SOPHIE
In a grid, rows are lines. So are columns, and diagonals. [[line: s05.b04.l01]]

A diagonal runs off the left edge and comes back in on the right: 6, 4, 9.

SOPHIE
This diagonal runs off the edge and comes back on the other side. [[line: s05.b04.l02]]

EULER
Like a clock with only three hours. [[line: s05.b04.l03]]

The top row and the left column light up together. [[beat: s05.b05]]

SOPHIE
A row plus a column makes a cross. [[line: s05.b05.l01]]

The other four points light up.

SOPHIE
And the four points left over make a square. [[line: s05.b05.l02]]

# s06. Four families

[[#6 | BEAT: Four families | OBJ: Sophie finds every hexad in the kitten; Ada adds them up | SETUP: none]]

A row and all three corners light up together. [[beat: s06.b01]]

SOPHIE
Now the hexads. One: a line, plus all three corners. Twelve of those. [[line: s06.b01.l01]]

Two rows light up. [[beat: s06.b02]]

SOPHIE
Two: a pair of parallel lines. Twelve more. [[line: s06.b02.l01]]

The cross lights up, and the corner above its grid. [[beat: s06.b03]]

SOPHIE
Three: a cross, plus its own corner. Eighteen crosses in each of three grids: fifty-four. [[line: s06.b03.l01]]

The square lights up, and the other two corners. [[beat: s06.b04]]

SOPHIE
Four: a square, plus the other two corners. Fifty-four again. [[line: s06.b04.l01]]

The four counts add up. [[beat: s06.b05]]

ADA
Twelve, twelve, fifty-four, fifty-four. A hundred thirty-two. [[line: s06.b05.l01]]

ADA
And I checked them against Euler's ring. The same hundred thirty-two. [[line: s06.b05.l02]]

SOPHIE
Two pictures, one object. [[line: s06.b05.l03]]

# s07. Back to the cards

[[#7 | BEAT: Back to the cards | OBJ: Sophie turns points into cards, and the losing hands light up | PAYOFF: special_hands]]

Card by card, the kitten's labels change: infinity becomes 0, 0 becomes 6, and so on. [[beat: s07.b01]]

SOPHIE
Now give every point a card value. Infinity becomes zero, zero becomes six, and the rest follow along. [[line: s07.b01.l01]]

SOPHIE
It's called the shuffle labeling. [[line: s07.b01.l02]]

The hand 1 to 6 appears above the kitten, and its cards light up in it. [[beat: s07.b02]]

SOPHIE
One to six. A square, plus corners one and six. A hexad! [[line: s07.b02.l01]]

The 45-hand lights up beside it.

EULER
And the forty-five is the cross in the same grid, plus corner zero. [[line: s07.b02.l02]]

Ada's 24-hand lights up, then the hand that ended the first game. [[beat: s07.b03]]

SOPHIE
Ada's twenty-four is a hexad. [[line: s07.b03.l01]]

SOPHIE
So is the end of our first game. [[line: s07.b03.l02]]

ADA
Every hand that lost is a hexad. [[line: s07.b03.l03]]

Euler leans in. [[beat: s07.b04]]

EULER
And the twenty-eight Pascal left Ada? [[line: s07.b04.l01]]

The 28-hand lights up: a square in the grid at 1, plus corners 0 and 6.

SOPHIE
A hexad too. [[line: s07.b04.l02]]

EULER
(pleased)
I guessed right. [[line: s07.b04.l03]]

PASCAL
I knew it all along. [[line: s07.b04.l04]]

Sophie looks from the kitten to the cards. [[beat: s07.b05]]

SOPHIE
So the special hands are hexads. But why do hexads lose? That, I can't see yet. [[line: s07.b05.l01]]

# s08. No memorizing

[[#8 | BEAT: No memorizing | OBJ: Pascal wants a shortcut; Ada refuses | SETUP: no_memorizing]]

Pascal rubs his paws together. [[beat: s08.b01]]

PASCAL
Great! We memorize all one hundred thirty-two, and I never lose again. [[line: s08.b01.l01]]

ADA
(deadpan)
Absolutely not. [[line: s08.b01.l02]]

PASCAL
Just the first fifty? [[line: s08.b01.l03]]

ADA
There's a better way. [[line: s08.b01.l04]]

